{
  "state": {
    "case_id": "367",
    "case_summary": "A 20-year-old woman was being monitored for a left ciliary body mass for 3 years presented with declining vision in her left eye. Her best-corrected visual acuity was 20/20 OD and 20/50 OS.\n\nPupils, intraocular pressures, anterior chambers, and lenses were unremarkable in both eyes. In the left eye, a nasal mass posterior to the lens was evident, which had grown considerably in size since her last visit. Ophthalmoscopic examination showed a large white mass protruding into the vitreous cavity.\n\n- Ultrasound biomicroscopy:  Image Title: Ultrasound biomicroscopic image  Image Description: Round, cystoid mass with a stalk attached to the nasal pars plana- Ultra-widefield imaging:  Image Title: Ultra-widefield fundus pseudocolor image  Image Description: White mass emanating anteriorly from the nasal ciliary body and protruding into the vitreous cavity; view to the optic nerve and macula is blocked- Histopathological examination:  - Vitreous aspirate cytology: Negative for malignant cells  - Microscopic findings: Hypovascular myxoid lesion composed of loosely arranged spindle and stellate cells with spindle nuclei. Hypocellular core with abundant myxoid stroma, relatively denser cellular area in cortex. No epithelial components, mitoses, or atypical cell-shape features- Immunohistochemical studies:  - S100 (neural crest marker): Positive  - Glial fibrillary acidic protein (astrocyte marker): Patchy positive  - CD34 (fibroblast marker): Negative  - Smooth muscle actin: Negative",
